{"type":"message","id":"07034a37","parentId":"e01b86ae","timestamp":"2026-05-18T12:56:21.388Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"[Mon 2026-05-18 12:56 UTC] [Subagent Context] You are running as a subagent (depth 1/1). Results auto-announce to your requester; do not busy-poll for status.\n\n[Subagent Task]: Test ONLY the image-generation path for the creator workflow. Try to generate a feature image using /tmp/research.json if available; if not, use a simple Bitcoin market-news scene. Save the final image to /tmp/crypto-feature.jpg if generation succeeds. In your final chat response, do NOT paste prompts or long logs. Instead, return a concise diagnostic report with: 1) whether generation succeeded, 2) which provider/model/path was attempted, 3) the exact failing step if it failed, and 4) whether /tmp/crypto-feature.jpg exists at the end. Keep it short and factual."}],"timestamp":1779108981364}}
{"type":"message","id":"85e82608","parentId":"07034a37","timestamp":"2026-05-18T12:56:24.813Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_jd1ieSnmhpPC5y0ilDqPw6gd","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"if [ -f /tmp/research.json ]; then echo मौजूद; cat /tmp/research.json; else echo MISSING; fi","workdir":"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-creator","yieldMs":10000,"timeout":30}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4-mini","usage":{"input":8191,"output":319,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":8510,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779108981391,"responseId":"chatcmpl-Dgro9Vq9KqhMwBxT3iiqnkxYfEptX"}}
{"type":"message","id":"8594b085","parentId":"85e82608","timestamp":"2026-05-18T12:56:25.943Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_jd1ieSnmhpPC5y0ilDqPw6gd","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"मौजूद\n{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"story_id\": \"bitcoin-bond-selloff-liquidations-2026-05-18\",\n  \"topic_theme\": \"Bitcoin Slides Below $77K Amid Bond Sell-Off and Macro Risk-Off\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"Bitcoin selloff\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"Bitcoin slides below $77,000 as Trump\\u2019s Iran warning rattles risk assets\",\n  \"primary_asset\": \"Bitcoin\",\n  \"chart_coin\": \"bitcoin\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"CoinDesk\",\n    \"Decrypt\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/18/bitcoin-slides-below-usd77-000-as-trump-s-iran-warning-rattles-risk-assets\",\n    \"https://decrypt.co/368156/bitcoin-slides-under-77k-as-crypto-liquidations-top-672m-amid-bond-sell-off\",\n    \"https://cointelegraph.com/markets/bitcoin-slides-below-79k-macro-fears-can-fixed-income-outflows-save-it?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"CoinDesk reported that Bitcoin slipped below $77,000 after President Trump\\u2019s Iran warning rattled risk assets, with oil moving higher and broad crypto liquidations following.\",\n    \"Decrypt said Bitcoin traded around $76,770 and that total crypto market liquidations exceeded $672 million, while the 10-year U.S. Treasury yield reached 4.63%, a 16-month high.\",\n    \"Decrypt also said U.S. spot Bitcoin ETFs posted $1 billion in net outflows for the week ending May 15, the largest weekly exit since late January, underscoring the institutional transmission channel from rates into crypto.\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph\\u2019s market roundup said Bitcoin started the week with a dip toward new May lows as ongoing BTC price pressure included collapsing U.S. bond markets and Iran-war uncertainty.\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"Bitcoin slid sharply at the start of the week as macro pressure, rising Treasury yields, and geopolitical anxiety hit risk assets across markets. CoinDesk described the move as Bitcoin falling below $77,000 after U.S. President Donald Trump warned Iran that the \\\"clock is ticking,\\\" a line that pushed oil higher and helped trigger broad crypto liquidations. The same feed also framed the day as a wider crypto unwind, noting that ether sank alongside Bitcoin. A separate CoinDesk headline tied the move to a broader liquidation wave, saying crypto traders betting on a rally lost $563 million in liquidations, with ether and Bitcoin suffering the most. Another CoinDesk market note said Bitcoin slid under $77,000 as oil shock and Treasury yields hit risk assets, pointing to the same macro transmission channel.\\n\\nDecrypt provided the deepest factual read on the move. Its article said Bitcoin fell to a three-week low over the weekend as a surge in U.S. Treasury yields sent spot Bitcoin ETF outflows to their worst level since late January and drove more than $670 million in liquidations across crypto markets. The piece said the 10-year U.S. Treasury yield reached 4.63% on Sunday night, the highest since February 2025, and that the move was up 70 basis points since the Iran war began. It added that odds of a 2026 rate cut had collapsed to 2%, reinforcing the idea that the bond market shock was cascading into crypto through expectations for tighter financial conditions. Decrypt quoted Diego Martin, CEO of Yellow Capital, saying geopolitical shocks no longer hit crypto directly the way they once did; instead, they hit Treasury yields, which hit risk appetite, which hits ETF flows, which hits Bitcoin. That same article said U.S. spot Bitcoin ETFs recorded $1 billion in net outflows for the week ending May 15, compared with $622.75 million in net inflows the prior week, and that the last comparable exit was the week of January 30, when $1.49 billion left the funds.\\n\\nDecrypt also anchored the price action with specific market levels. It said Bitcoin was trading around $76,770 at the time of writing and that total crypto liquidations had exceeded $672 million according to CoinGlass. The article said the $77,000 level was the key line to watch and warned that if it broke while perpetual swap open interest remained elevated, a retest of $70,000 or lower could become a realistic scenario rather than a tail risk. Georgii Verbitski, a derivatives trader and founder of TYMIO, told Decrypt that Bitcoin\\u2019s near-term trajectory depended heavily on the AI-driven equity rally holding up, because the market lacked a strong standalone demand driver at current levels. The story also noted that Bitcoin\\u2019s muted recovery came after its lackluster reaction to the CLARITY Act advancing out of the Senate Banking Committee.\\n\\nCoinTelegraph\\u2019s coverage aligned with the same macro theme. One article called the setup a possible \\\"bull trap\\\" at $76.5K and said Bitcoin started the week with a dip toward new May lows as ongoing BTC price pressures included collapsing U.S. bond markets. Another market piece said Bitcoin slid below $79K on macro fears, with Iran war uncertainty and fixed-income market dislocation weighing on the price. CoinTelegraph also ran a bear-trap analysis saying Bitcoin had fallen below $78,000 for the first time since the start of May, but traders were still hoping for a rebound. These pieces help confirm that the market-wide selloff was not isolated to one outlet\\u2019s framing: the move was being discussed across multiple crypto desks as a combined rates, geopolitics, and liquidation event.\\n\\nThe shared picture across the sources is a risk-off crypto episode driven by three linked forces: higher Treasury yields, Iran-related geopolitical stress, and forced unwinding of leveraged positions. Bitcoin\\u2019s break below $77,000 became the focal point because it aligned with large ETF outflows, elevated liquidations, and weakening support from other macro-risk assets. Even though some analysts still characterized the move as potentially temporary or a bear trap, all three sources pointed to the same immediate reality: Bitcoin was under pressure from the bond market first, and crypto sentiment was following that lead.\"\n}"}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":61,"aggregated":"मौजूद\n{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"story_id\": \"bitcoin-bond-selloff-liquidations-2026-05-18\",\n  \"topic_theme\": \"Bitcoin Slides Below $77K Amid Bond Sell-Off and Macro Risk-Off\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"Bitcoin selloff\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"Bitcoin slides below $77,000 as Trump\\u2019s Iran warning rattles risk assets\",\n  \"primary_asset\": \"Bitcoin\",\n  \"chart_coin\": \"bitcoin\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"CoinDesk\",\n    \"Decrypt\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/18/bitcoin-slides-below-usd77-000-as-trump-s-iran-warning-rattles-risk-assets\",\n    \"https://decrypt.co/368156/bitcoin-slides-under-77k-as-crypto-liquidations-top-672m-amid-bond-sell-off\",\n    \"https://cointelegraph.com/markets/bitcoin-slides-below-79k-macro-fears-can-fixed-income-outflows-save-it?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"CoinDesk reported that Bitcoin slipped below $77,000 after President Trump\\u2019s Iran warning rattled risk assets, with oil moving higher and broad crypto liquidations following.\",\n    \"Decrypt said Bitcoin traded around $76,770 and that total crypto market liquidations exceeded $672 million, while the 10-year U.S. Treasury yield reached 4.63%, a 16-month high.\",\n    \"Decrypt also said U.S. spot Bitcoin ETFs posted $1 billion in net outflows for the week ending May 15, the largest weekly exit since late January, underscoring the institutional transmission channel from rates into crypto.\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph\\u2019s market roundup said Bitcoin started the week with a dip toward new May lows as ongoing BTC price pressure included collapsing U.S. bond markets and Iran-war uncertainty.\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"Bitcoin slid sharply at the start of the week as macro pressure, rising Treasury yields, and geopolitical anxiety hit risk assets across markets. CoinDesk described the move as Bitcoin falling below $77,000 after U.S. President Donald Trump warned Iran that the \\\"clock is ticking,\\\" a line that pushed oil higher and helped trigger broad crypto liquidations. The same feed also framed the day as a wider crypto unwind, noting that ether sank alongside Bitcoin. A separate CoinDesk headline tied the move to a broader liquidation wave, saying crypto traders betting on a rally lost $563 million in liquidations, with ether and Bitcoin suffering the most. Another CoinDesk market note said Bitcoin slid under $77,000 as oil shock and Treasury yields hit risk assets, pointing to the same macro transmission channel.\\n\\nDecrypt provided the deepest factual read on the move. Its article said Bitcoin fell to a three-week low over the weekend as a surge in U.S. Treasury yields sent spot Bitcoin ETF outflows to their worst level since late January and drove more than $670 million in liquidations across crypto markets. The piece said the 10-year U.S. Treasury yield reached 4.63% on Sunday night, the highest since February 2025, and that the move was up 70 basis points since the Iran war began. It added that odds of a 2026 rate cut had collapsed to 2%, reinforcing the idea that the bond market shock was cascading into crypto through expectations for tighter financial conditions. Decrypt quoted Diego Martin, CEO of Yellow Capital, saying geopolitical shocks no longer hit crypto directly the way they once did; instead, they hit Treasury yields, which hit risk appetite, which hits ETF flows, which hits Bitcoin. That same article said U.S. spot Bitcoin ETFs recorded $1 billion in net outflows for the week ending May 15, compared with $622.75 million in net inflows the prior week, and that the last comparable exit was the week of January 30, when $1.49 billion left the funds.\\n\\nDecrypt also anchored the price action with specific market levels. It said Bitcoin was trading around $76,770 at the time of writing and that total crypto liquidations had exceeded $672 million according to CoinGlass. The article said the $77,000 level was the key line to watch and warned that if it broke while perpetual swap open interest remained elevated, a retest of $70,000 or lower could become a realistic scenario rather than a tail risk. Georgii Verbitski, a derivatives trader and founder of TYMIO, told Decrypt that Bitcoin\\u2019s near-term trajectory depended heavily on the AI-driven equity rally holding up, because the market lacked a strong standalone demand driver at current levels. The story also noted that Bitcoin\\u2019s muted recovery came after its lackluster reaction to the CLARITY Act advancing out of the Senate Banking Committee.\\n\\nCoinTelegraph\\u2019s coverage aligned with the same macro theme. One article called the setup a possible \\\"bull trap\\\" at $76.5K and said Bitcoin started the week with a dip toward new May lows as ongoing BTC price pressures included collapsing U.S. bond markets. Another market piece said Bitcoin slid below $79K on macro fears, with Iran war uncertainty and fixed-income market dislocation weighing on the price. CoinTelegraph also ran a bear-trap analysis saying Bitcoin had fallen below $78,000 for the first time since the start of May, but traders were still hoping for a rebound. These pieces help confirm that the market-wide selloff was not isolated to one outlet\\u2019s framing: the move was being discussed across multiple crypto desks as a combined rates, geopolitics, and liquidation event.\\n\\nThe shared picture across the sources is a risk-off crypto episode driven by three linked forces: higher Treasury yields, Iran-related geopolitical stress, and forced unwinding of leveraged positions. Bitcoin\\u2019s break below $77,000 became the focal point because it aligned with large ETF outflows, elevated liquidations, and weakening support from other macro-risk assets. Even though some analysts still characterized the move as potentially temporary or a bear trap, all three sources pointed to the same immediate reality: Bitcoin was under pressure from the bond market first, and crypto sentiment was following that lead.\"\n}","cwd":"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-creator"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1779108985943}}
